Level8 walkthrough

Program flow
- The program loops forever and prints two pointers with printf("%p, %p ", g_service, g_auth).
- It reads a line with fgets into a 0x80-byte buffer.
- It checks the start of the line against four commands:
	- "auth ": allocate 4 bytes for g_auth, zero them, then copy the text after "auth " into g_auth if the length is <= 0x1e.
	- "reset": free(g_auth).
	- "service": g_service = strdup(input + 7).
	- "login": if *(int *)(g_auth + 0x20) is non-zero, system("/bin/sh"); otherwise print "Password:".

What *(int *)(g_auth + 0x20) means
- g_auth is a char*.
- g_auth + 0x20 moves 32 bytes forward.
- (int *) casts that address to an int*.
- *(int *) dereferences and reads the 4-byte integer at that address.

Why it is exploitable
- g_auth only points to a 4-byte allocation, but strcpy can copy up to 30 bytes into it.
- This overflows into adjacent heap memory.
- The login check reads 4 bytes at offset 0x20 from g_auth, so an overflow can make this value non-zero.

Why g_auth + 0x20 often lands in g_service
- It is not guaranteed by C, but it often happens with glibc’s heap layout.
- The next heap chunk frequently sits right after the tiny g_auth chunk.
- If the next chunk is the "service" buffer, the read at g_auth + 0x20 falls inside that buffer and sees non-zero data.

Example input sequence
auth A
service AAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAA
login

$ cat /home/user/level9/.pass
c542e581c5ba5162a85f767996e3247ed619ef6c6f7b76a59435545dc6259f8a

This usually makes the login check pass because the service buffer fills the bytes read at g_auth + 0x20.
